      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "A related useful setting (that might be less niche) would be the ability to make the detail mask use the same UV set as the detail map, rather than the main texture. Potentially useful for decal-like details, so you can keep the mask on the smaller detail texture.\n\nDidn't include it originally, because I ended up just swapping my main and detail textures, so I thought it was identical. However, the semantics are slightly different. I'm achieving effectively a \"replacement\" of the color, whereas the other would be a more traditional multiplicative detail.",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    }
  ],
  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
  "created": "2024-06-09T20:25:20.957Z",
  "deletedAt": null,
  "deletedBy": null,
  "details": "What I'm defining as a UV set is the combination of UV map, tiling and offset. Standard Lite provides 2: texcoord0 and texcoord1, where the main textures and the detail mask use texcoord0 and the detail textures use texcoord1.\n\nI'd like the (admittedly niche) ability to have the detail textures also use texcoord0, while detail mask uses texcoord1. This would allow for an effect similar to lerping between two textures based on a third texture, and you can scroll things to implement some effects without needing additional textures or materials.\n\nMy (potential) usecase for this is using the main + detail albedo as a set of palette textures, and the detail mask + UV map to encode *which* color to use. This would allow me to (mostly) recreate the visible impact of an effect I've been using on the PC version of my avatar. (it occurs to me belatedly that I'd still want the detail normal to be using texcoord1 in this case, but i could live without it in the interest of reducing shader variants or runtime checks)",
